$p'=(\x_1',\dots,\x_{j-1}')$, with both prefixes corresponding to the same $h$,   the corresponding rows fulfill the same conditions, namely  that $q_t(\x_k)=q_t(\x_k')=h_k$ and $\x_k\cdot\x_l=\x_k'\cdot\x_l'=h_{kl}$. Thus, the linear map $\phi$ that sends $\mathbf 1_t$ to $\mathbf 1_t$ and sends each $\x_k$ to $\x_k'$ preserves the quadratic and bilinear structure, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:fiber} we can take it (or, more precisely, some extension of it to a map on all of $\mathbb{F}_2^t$) to be represented by a global $q_t$-isometry $g\in G_t = \{g\in GL_t(\mathbb{F}_2):q_t(g\x)=q_t(\x) \quad \forall \x\in \mathbb{F}_2^t\}$.
