\end{equation}
All the constraints defining $L_{\s}$ are affine linear in the unknown vector $\x$, as the previous rows $\x_k$ as well as the $h_{kj}$ are already fixed. If we then use Gaussian elimination on this linear system of constraints, we either get that the system is unsolvable and $N_{\s}'=0$, or Gaussian elimination gives a particular solution $\x_0$ and a basis $\boldsymbol{v}_1,\dots,\boldsymbol{v}_m$ for the homogeneous solution space, meaning $|L_{\s}|=2^m$. Every vector in $L_{\s}$ can then be written uniquely as
